.TH MEMSPYD 8 "October 2026" "memspy 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
memspyd \- detect high-confidence fileless malware behavior with eBPF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B memspyd
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B memspyd
loads eBPF probes, evaluates local detection rules, retains alerts for 30 days, publishes structured journal records, and exposes a local Unix socket API. Optional BPF LSM response denies supported memory-backed or deleted-image execution and cross-process memory modification primitives. It does not kill, quarantine, inspect payload memory, or open a network listener.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --config " PATH"
Use an exact-path allowlist other than /etc/memspy/allowlist.conf.
.TP
.BI --database " PATH"
Use an alert database other than /var/lib/memspy/alerts.db.
.TP
.BI --socket " PATH"
Use a Unix socket other than /run/memspy/memspy.sock.
.TP
.B --foreground
Run in the foreground. This is the packaged default.
.TP
.B --check-response
Check whether detection and BPF LSM response are available, then exit.
.TP
.B --version
Print the version.
.TP
.B --help
Print command help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/memspy/allowlist.conf
One exact rule ID and absolute executable path per line.
.TP
.I /var/lib/memspy/alerts.db
Root-owned SQLite alert history, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /run/memspy/memspy.sock
Local API, mode 0660 and group memspy-ui. Response changes require PolicyKit authorization.
.SH ALERT OUTPUT
Every stored alert is also submitted to journald with stable MEMSPY_RULE, MEMSPY_SEVERITY, MEMSPY_ACTION, MEMSPY_PID, MEMSPY_UID, MEMSPY_ALERT_ID, and MEMSPY_ALERT_JSON fields. Journal failure degrades health without blocking storage or API publication.
.SH COVERAGE
PROC_MEM_WRITE is emitted only after a successful write through a procfs process-memory descriptor validated in eBPF. MemSpy records the host TGID and namespace-visible PID; process exit or host-PID reuse before enrichment produces an incomplete observation rather than a guessed alert. Process, parent, workload, container, terminal, SSH-origin, and namespace evidence is bounded and may be unavailable for short-lived processes. Applicable actor, executed-image, target, pre-exec launcher, and parent images are hashed in one pass up to 128 MiB. SHA-256 is primary and MD5 is retained for IOC compatibility; exact failure statuses are stored when hashing is not possible.
.SH STORAGE FAILURE
If the SQLite database cannot open, the service preserves it, continues bounded live detection in degraded health, and retries storage every 60 seconds.
.SH SECURITY
The service requires x86_64 Linux 5.4 or newer and readable kernel BTF at /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux. Add only trusted administrators to memspy-ui. Membership permits reading alert metadata from all monitored users. Canonical alert JSON in journald can include command lines and process paths, but never memory payloads or environment variables.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR memspy-ui (1),
.BR memspyctl (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
